  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nIncreasing cumulus clouds and a slight haze high in the sky will\nbe the main weather story for this afternoon and evening as \nwildfire smoke drifts overhead. Despite the haze, it will be a \nvery pleasant and comfortable day across the Northland. \nAfternoon highs will reach the middle 70s to the lower 80s, and \nwith humidity remaining relatively low.\n\nThe International Border will likely see some high-based showers\npassing through that area of far northern MN this afternoon to\nearly evening, but very dry low-levels limits much of any precip\nfrom reaching the ground and to virga. The forecast grids for \nthat area this afternoon remain without any weather mentions for\nthis case.\n\nA weak shortwave rotating through a deeper vertically stacked\nHudson Bay Low pressure skirts through southeast Manitoba and\nnorthwest Ontario tonight. This will bring a band of high-based \nrain showers into northeast Minnesota late this evening, through\nthe overnight and into early Sunday morning. If you live along \nand north of the Iron Range into the Arrowhead, you could see \nthe highest rainfall totals, localized up to half an inch. Late\ntonight into Sunday morning, mid-level instability builds into\ncentral MN and northwest Wisconsin on the nose of an incoming \nlow-level jet. This setup may (20-30% chance) help trigger a \nfew non-severe isolated thunderstorms across east-central MN \nand northwest Wisconsin through Sunday morning. This will also\nbe a short period of increased dewpoints early Sunday that keeps\na wetting rainfall around a quarter-inch of localized rainfall\npossible for northwest Wisconsin. Severe weather is not \nexpected tonight into Sunday morning.\n\nLooking ahead to next week, the Northland will remain on the\ncooler side of a stalled weather front located to our south. \nThis setup will keep our temperatures near to slightly below \nnormal going into the early part of mid-August. The weather \npattern will also keep with the pattern of a weak system passing\nthrough the Upper Midwest and helping create a 10 to 30 percent\nchance of scattered showers and thunderstorms every day or two.\nThe severe weather threat though is expected to stay focused \nwell to our south across southern Minnesota and southern \nWisconsin.",
